#{[#uuid "882aa12a-1be9-5fa0-8b2f-2a42007270f7" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060955 :tutorial/content "The **constant multiple rule** states that if a function $f(x)$ is multiplied by a *constant* factor $k$, then in order to differentiate $kf(x)$ we can take the derivative of $f(x)$ and then multiply by $k$ afterwards:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(k f(x)\\right) = k \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}\\left(f(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, to differentiate $y=6x^2$, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(6x^{2}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot 2x \\\\\n&= 12x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "882aa12a-1be9-5fa0-8b2f-2a42007270f7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 345 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [#uuid "a136fcee-de1f-5f62-b419-01f7fc6b3279" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097939 :tutorial/content "Often, differential equations aren't easy to solve. Some are so difficult that they can only be solved using entire rooms full of very powerful computers! \n\nHowever, there is one type that's often quite easy. These are differential equations that can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = g(x).\n$$\n\nAs an example, suppose that we want to find the solution to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{1+2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this differential equation by integrating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\dfrac{1}{1+2x}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nFor the left-hand side, calculating the indefinite integral of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ gives $y+C_1$ because differentiation is reversed by integration. For the right-hand side, this is a reasonably standard integral that we know how to compute. So, we get\n\n$$\ny + C_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln|1+2x| + C_2.\n$$\n\nWe now tidy up the result, solving for $y$ and combining the two constants $C_1$ and $C_2$ into a third constant $C=C_2-C_1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+{C}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}|1+2x|+{C}_{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}|1+2x|+({C}_{2}-{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}|1+2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution we obtained is called a **general solution** because it involves the arbitrary constant $C.$ This is in contrast to \"particular\" solutions, in which the constant is set to a fixed value. We will learn more about particular solutions later, but for now we will focus on general solutions.\n\n**Note:** Remember that we combined the constants $C_1$ and $C_2$ into a single constant $C.$ For these types of differential equations, we can usually do this, and for that reason, we often go straight to adding a constant $C$ to one side of the equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "a136fcee-de1f-5f62-b419-01f7fc6b3279" :tutorial/math-academy-id 952 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [#uuid "a6a381ed-e9ab-5deb-9d74-30900610edf1" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088866 :tutorial/content "Given a first-order linear ODE, we can find an integrating factor and use it to solve the equation using the following procedure.\n\n- **Step 1**: Write the equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x} \\,.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3**: Multiply both sides of the equation found in step 1 by $I(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nI(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + I(x)P(x)y = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 4**: Rewrite the left side as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y I(x)\\Bigr)\n$$\n\nIf steps 1 through 3 are done correctly, then we can always do this, and we can check that it's correct by differentiating. Our new equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y I(x)\\Bigr) = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 5**: Integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$ and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ yI(x) & =\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{I(x)}(\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou might be wondering where the formula for $I(x)$ comes from; we'll discuss this at the end of the lesson. First, let's apply this method to a concrete example in the next slide." :tutorial/id #uuid "a6a381ed-e9ab-5deb-9d74-30900610edf1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14551 :tutorial/title "The Integrating Factor Method"}] [#uuid "a71b1a8d-93f4-5e48-ab3a-bd7220d50f18" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097819 :tutorial/content "The exponential function  $f(x) = e^x$ has the special property that its derivative is the function itself:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( e^x \\right)= e^x\n$$\n\nSo, taking the derivative of an exponential function does nothing to the exponential function. The exponential function remains exactly the same. \n\nNote that the exponential function is the *only* function with this special property.\n\nWe'll justify this result at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice working with it." :tutorial/id #uuid "a71b1a8d-93f4-5e48-ab3a-bd7220d50f18" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2611 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [#uuid "b1f46211-3766-53d0-bcc7-30bb9056b948" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097822 :tutorial/content "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\ln x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this result in a future lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "b1f46211-3766-53d0-bcc7-30bb9056b948" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2615 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [#uuid "b27482c3-602c-5761-9cd5-bec55b2c2932" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102037 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nHow can we verify whether a given function is a **solution** of this equation? For example, is $y=e^{2x}$ a solution?\n\nTo check whether a given function $y$ satisfies a differential equation, we calculate the derivative and plug the result into the equation.\n\nFor $y=e^{2x},$ the derivative is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2e^{2x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now substitute the above and our expression for $y,$ into the original equation and see if we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y \\\\ {{2{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{{y}^{'}} & =2\\cdot {{{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{y} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get a true statement! Therefore, $y=e^{2x}$ is a solution to the original equation.\n\n**Note:** There are often infinitely many solutions to a given differential equation! We will learn how to solve differential equations like the one shown above in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "b27482c3-602c-5761-9cd5-bec55b2c2932" :tutorial/math-academy-id 947 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [#uuid "bfa0c70d-6cf7-5c69-8995-d9f6c980a4ba" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065415 :tutorial/content "We know that the derivative of sine is cosine:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(\\sin{x}) = \\cos{x}\n$$\n\nSince integration is the reverse of differentiation, we see that the integral of cosine is sine:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos{x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\sin{x} + C\n$$\n\nWe can discover two other integrals by considering the derivatives of cosine and tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cos}x+C \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{tan}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the derivatives of secant, cosecant, and cotangent, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sec}x+C \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}x+C \\\\ \\int {csc}^{2}x\\text{ }\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{cot}x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith these six formulas, we can integrate many different trigonometric functions." :tutorial/id #uuid "bfa0c70d-6cf7-5c69-8995-d9f6c980a4ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 718 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [#uuid "c0803a18-b9e8-5c2e-90a8-6f8946cd5d0a" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168090 :tutorial/content "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be **autonomous** if it can be written in the form \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),\n$$\n\nor equivalently, \n\n$$\ny'=f(y).\n$$\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -5y^2\n$$\n\nis autonomous, while the equation \n\n$$\ny' = \\dfrac{y+2}{x^2}\n$$\n\nis *not* autonomous since the expression for $y'$ on the right-hand side contains the independent variable $x$ explicitly, outside the function $y=y(x).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c0803a18-b9e8-5c2e-90a8-6f8946cd5d0a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5169 :tutorial/title "Autonomous Ordinary Differential Equations"}] [#uuid "cc4983db-1d7c-5573-a6d1-9e5e4a09207e" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088950 :tutorial/content "A **first-order linear ordinary differential equation** is a differential equation in the variable $y(x)$ that can be expressed in the **general form**\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $a(x),$ $b(x),$ and $f(x)$ are functions of $x$ only, and\n\n- $a(x)$ is not identically zero (i.e., it is not zero for all $x$).\n\nFor example, the differential equation\n\n$$\nxy' + e^x y=1\n$$\n\nis a first-order linear ODE with $a(x) = x, b(x) = e^x,$ and $f(x) = 1.$\n\nOn the other hand, the differential equation \n\n$$\nx(y')^2 +  e^x \\sin(y)=1\n$$\n\nis *not* a first-order linear ODE. Notice that it contains the terms $(y')^2$ and $\\sin(y),$ and therefore cannot be presented in the general form." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc4983db-1d7c-5573-a6d1-9e5e4a09207e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4055 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [#uuid "d0517ea0-b60e-5c34-bfbc-41fd6c61af75" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088951 :tutorial/content "The general form of a first-order linear ODE is\n\n$$\na(x)y'+b(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nNow, if we take the general form and divide both sides by $a(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a(x)}{a(x)}{y}^{'}+\\frac{b(x)}{a(x)}y & =\\frac{f(x)}{a(x)} \\\\ {y}^{'}+\\frac{b(x)}{a(x)}y & =\\frac{f(x)}{a(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that $a(x)$ is not identically zero, so we're allowed to perform this division.\n\nNow, if we define the functions $P$ and $Q$ as \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\dfrac{b(x)}{a(x)}, \\qquad Q(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{a(x)},\n$$\n\n \n\nthen we can write our equation in the following **standard form**:\n\n$$\ny'+P(x)y =Q(x)\n$$\n\nFor example, given the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\ny' \\sin x+ y e^x= \\cos x,\n$$\n\nthe standard form of this equation can be found by dividing through by $\\sin x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}+y(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{sin}x}) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\ {y}^{'}+y{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}x & =\\operatorname{cot}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nP(x) = e^x \\csc x, \\qquad Q(x) = \\cot x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d0517ea0-b60e-5c34-bfbc-41fd6c61af75" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4056 :tutorial/title "The Standard Form of a First-Order Linear ODE"}] [#uuid "061a5c54-700a-5793-b266-cf156584544a" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097820 :tutorial/content "If we replace  the base $e$ of an exponential function by any other positive number $a$, then the derivative is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( a^x \\right)= a^x \\ln a\n$$\n\nWe just multiply the given exponential function by the natural logarithm of the base.\n\nNote that since $\\ln e =1$, our first formula is a particular case of this one!\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( e^x \\right)= e^x \\ln e = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "061a5c54-700a-5793-b266-cf156584544a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2612 :tutorial/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Base Other Than e"}] [#uuid "1066a4d7-d790-5316-9925-1ac0e928ce01" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088865 :tutorial/content "Recall that a first-order linear ODE can always be expressed in the standard form\n\n$$\ny'(x)+P(x)y(x) =Q(x).\n$$\n\nFirst-order linear ODEs are special because we can always find the general solution using an **integrating factor.** \n\nAn integrating factor is a function $I(x)$ that allows us to rewrite the left-hand side as an exact derivative after multiplying the equation by $I(x).$ The resulting equation can then be solved by integrating both sides.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = 3\n$$\n\nAs it stands, this differential equation cannot be integrated directly. However, if we multiply both sides of the equation by $I(x) = x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nx^2\\cdot \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^2\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = x^2\\cdot 3\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\nx^2 \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + 2 x y = 3x^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, here's the neat part. Provided our integrating factor is correct, we can *always* write the left-hand side of the equation as the exact derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich in our case is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y \\cdot x^2\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2.\n$$\n\nWe can now find the general solution by integrating both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y\\cdot {x}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+C \\\\ y=x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2\n$$\n\nis indeed equivalent to the original equation $(\\ast)$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {x}^{2}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =3{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy finding an appropriate integrating factor, we can solve any first-order linear ODE! So the question is, how do we find an integrating factor? Let's discuss this next." :tutorial/id #uuid "1066a4d7-d790-5316-9925-1ac0e928ce01" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14550 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [#uuid "1b006e01-04f6-5f6b-b435-b0f89163d7a1" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097821 :tutorial/content "To derive the identity\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x,\n$$\n\nwe begin with the definition of the derivative:\n\nwhere $f(x) = e^x$.\n\nSubstituting the function $f(x)$ into the definition of $f'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x+h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{h}-{e}^{x}}{h} \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot ({e}^{h}-1)}{h} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{h}-1}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{h \\to 0} \\frac{e^h - 1}{h} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nf'(x) = e^x \\cdot 1 = e^x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(e^x) = e^x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1b006e01-04f6-5f6b-b435-b0f89163d7a1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2613 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Rule for the Derivative of the Exponential Function"}] [#uuid "2107c0cc-b904-547f-b13d-9a0898b33d73" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088868 :tutorial/content "Recall that the standard form of a first-order linear differential equation is\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that $Q(x)$ is called the **forcing function.**\n\nA first-order linear ODE is **homogeneous** if the forcing function $Q(x) = 0$ for all $x.$ In other words, the equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear ODE is **inhomogeneous** if it is not homogeneous.\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- Suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\ny' - xy = 0.\n$$\n\nThe equation matches the form $y' + P(x)y = 0$ with $P(x) = -x.$ Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Now, consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' - xy = 6.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $Q(x) = 6$ means that the equation cannot be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is *not* homogeneous (i.e., it is *inhomogeneous*).\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + xy = 2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left-hand side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}+xy & =2y \\\\ {y}^{'}+xy-2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{'}+(x-2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form $y' + P(x)y = 0$ with $P(x) = (x-2).$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous." :tutorial/id #uuid "2107c0cc-b904-547f-b13d-9a0898b33d73" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14653 :tutorial/title "Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Equations"}] [#uuid "3c3298ec-d6a5-5792-9ebc-90f1b44de891" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097940 :tutorial/content "Often, instead of the general solution that involves an arbitrary constant, we want a **particular solution**. A particular solution is a solution that satisfies the differential equation *and* some other condition on the function.  \n\nAs a simple example, consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = 2x.\n$$\n\nWe can find the general solution by integrating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn its current form, the function $y$ represents infinitely many solutions, each with a different value of the arbitrary constant $C.$ \n\nHowever, suppose we want to find a **particular solution** that satisfies the condition $y(3)=10.$ To do this, we can solve for the appropriate value of the arbitrary constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}+C \\\\ 10 & ={3}^{2}+C \\\\ 10 & =9+C \\\\ 1 & =C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired particular solution is\n\n$$\ny = x^2 + 1.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c3298ec-d6a5-5792-9ebc-90f1b44de891" :tutorial/math-academy-id 957 :tutorial/title "Particular Solutions of a Differential Equation"}] [#uuid "46723f7e-094f-5e9c-af08-e9e7c0b4a88e" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168091 :tutorial/content "A first-order **initial value problem** (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x(2x^2-y') = -1}, \\qquad {\\color{red}y(e)=-2}\n$$\n\nis a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}x(2x^2-y') = -1}\n$$\n\ntogether with the initial condition \n\n$$\n{\\color{red} y(e)=-2}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "46723f7e-094f-5e9c-af08-e9e7c0b4a88e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5170 :tutorial/title "First-Order Initial Value Problems"}] [#uuid "4d012836-922f-5da1-92d2-1a48b741d1ae" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168075 :tutorial/content "An **ordinary differential equation** is an equation that involves a function with one variable (usually denoted as $y$), its derivatives, and an independent variable (usually denoted as $x$).\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}-3xy^2 = y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nis an ordinary differential equation (ODE). \n\n**Watch out!** Here, the function $y=y(x)$ actually depends on the independent variable $x.$\n\nThe **order of an ordinary differential equation** is the order of the highest derivative in the equation. \n\nIn our example above, the highest derivative is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2},\n$$\n\nwhich has order $2.$ Therefore, the order of the ODE is $2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4d012836-922f-5da1-92d2-1a48b741d1ae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5014 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [#uuid "551a7005-80c0-5cee-b28e-f3b565aaf250" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097812 :tutorial/content "Now, let's derive the rule\n![](images/77/77334bed6aafc3383552d90bcfc8da614d56493edce4e7ab89a2f28e01d3dbfa.svg)\n\nThe trick here is to write this in terms of ![](images/5b/5ba79af546498192229d0cdd5a4a922d1d015802402b8eec5910fe00f999820d.svg) \n\nFirst, note that since the natural logarithm is the inverse of the exponential function, we have\n![](images/09/091c69568c8472aacf8229a756a835e0edc26700677e4fa67de3d2f6ac0d96a2.svg)\n\nand by the power rule for logarithms, we can write this as\n\n![](images/c9/c9b146d550ef068823a4ee4b9f63eea89f46d6ceecedf0ad4baa6462f11d9127.svg)\n\nNow, the following result can be used (we'll prove it in the subsequent topics):\n\n![](images/ce/cedfc7ab127ac8b34cd72b21e8c3e527541e054721324d9d948d6df123cd61e0.svg)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n![](images/58/5880fb665abda7f3cef4ad7425c99c43b16e84c448c7b9e22ff32f76e36df87c.svg)\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "551a7005-80c0-5cee-b28e-f3b565aaf250" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13257 :tutorial/title "Justifying the Second Rule"}] [#uuid "743ad997-8732-5c0f-9676-ff1d44f3b513" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097878 :tutorial/content "The **chain rule** for differentiation states that the derivative of a composite function $f(g(x))$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}f} {\\textrm{d}g} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}g} {\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nThere is some intuition behind this rule: if we think of the derivatives as fractions, the ${\\textrm{d}g}$'s would appear to cancel out.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{d}g}}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see how the chain rule can be used to differentiate a function like \n\n$$\nf(x) = (1+2x)^3,\n$$\n\nfirst, notice that the function can be written as a composite function\n\n$$\nf(g) = g^3, \\qquad g(x) = 1+2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}g}({g}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+2x) \\\\  & =3{g}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6{g}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(x) = 1+2x$ for $g,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6(1+2x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We'll provide a derivation of the chain rule at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "743ad997-8732-5c0f-9676-ff1d44f3b513" :tutorial/math-academy-id 415 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [#uuid "7a7450e4-ec0e-5a5e-9c86-9430eb889be7" {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097823 :tutorial/content "For a general logarithm of base $a,$ the derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\log_a x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln a}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that whenever $\\log$ is written without a base, we can assume that it represents the base $10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\log x =  \\log_{10} x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(\\operatorname{log}x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({log}_{10}x)=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** To see where this rule comes from, we can use the change-of-base formula as shown below.\n\n$$\n\\log_a x = \\dfrac{\\ln x}{\\ln a}\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative and using the fact that $\\ln a$ is a constant, then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{\\operatorname{ln}a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}a}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}a}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7a7450e4-ec0e-5a5e-9c86-9430eb889be7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2616 :tutorial/title "Differentiating a Logarithmic Function with a General Base"}]}
